Author: henry <henrychen917@protonmail.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 28 16:02:10 2026 +0800

    snapfix: size each snapshot's fresh table from the live set, not from cap_[0] (SNAP-STORM-1)
    
    FlatStore::snapshot_prepare allocated the post-cut table at twice the CURRENT capacity. After the
    capture the frozen table merges into it and nothing shrinks it again (no deletes, no shrink), so
    every BGSAVE doubled every shard's hash table for good: an idle one-key server went from 20 MB to
    13 GB RSS over 16 BGSAVEs (PRE and POST alike), and each next Preparing first had to finish
    merging the previous, already doubled, table eight slots per rehash step -- seconds per BGSAVE
    start after ~10 saves, while the armed atomic barrier held every new EXEC/script unit and plain
    writers kept going. That is the BGSAVE-loop signature at412 reported (cuts armed == saves, BGSAVE
    in progress, units stuck); the at412 script + writer diagnostic still reproduced it on the first
    POST after ~10 saves in all four mode x engine cells.
    
    Fix: twice the capacity the ordinary resize policy needs for the live set (smallest power of two
    from kMinCap with load below kLoadPct), never more than the old rule. A table sitting at that
    minimal size gets exactly the old size; a sparser one gets less, still at least 1.86x the live
    set of post-cut room. The post-cut insert gate and the merge are unchanged. Idle BGSAVE loop: RSS
    flat at ~21 MB; at412 script + writer diagnostic: 0 of 20 attempts reproduce.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0131nXyQ5SvYWSgqqkHefHVb

diff --git a/src/store/flatstore.h b/src/store/flatstore.h
index 69d24873c..f70b2ae4c 100644
--- a/src/store/flatstore.h
+++ b/src/store/flatstore.h
@@ -1032,7 +1032,16 @@ public:
         if (snapshot_prepared_) return SnapshotWriteResult::Ready;
         if (read_local_enabled_ && !prepare_resize_retirement())
             return SnapshotWriteResult::Error;
-        uint64_t wanted = static_cast<uint64_t>(cap_[0]) * 2;
+        // Twice the capacity the resize policy needs for the LIVE set (load below kLoadPct), not
+        // twice cap_[0] (SNAP-STORM-1). After the capture the frozen table merges into this one and
+        // nothing shrinks it again, so sizing it from cap_[0] compounded: every snapshot doubled
+        // every shard's table for good (an idle one-key server passed 13 GB RSS after 16 BGSAVEs),
+        // and each next Preparing first had to finish merging the previous, already doubled,
+        // table eight slots per step while the armed atomic barrier held every new unit. The
+        // result is still overprovisioned for post-cut inserts, which insert() capacity-gates.
+        uint64_t sized = kMinCap;
+        while ((static_cast<uint64_t>(live_[0]) + 1) * 100 >= sized * kLoadPct) sized *= 2;
+        uint64_t wanted = std::min<uint64_t>(sized, cap_[0]) * 2;
         if (wanted > UINT32_MAX) return SnapshotWriteResult::Error;
         const uint32_t cap = round_pow2(static_cast<uint32_t>(wanted));
         if (!cap) return SnapshotWriteResult::Error;
